\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Scrambling and search functions}

\p{Random moves raise a different question from solving: when, and how
quickly, does a scramble lose information about its starting state? Before
choosing a search method, we need a model of that information loss. A
\newvocab{random walk} depends on its allowed moves and their probabilities.
Functions on the move group can measure persistent information;
representation theory provides coordinates for such functions and for the
action of averaging over moves. Pan and Kondor use a Fourier basis to describe
functions that guide \newvocab{search} on permutation puzzles
\citet{Sections 4--4.1}{pan2021fourier}.}

\p{A useful learned \newvocab{search score} need not be a lower bound on
solution length. Pan and Kondor explicitly distinguish their scores from admissible
heuristics \citet{Section 6}{pan2021fourier}.
Likewise, a long random move sequence need not be a uniform scramble.
Analyzing its distribution and proving guarantees for a search procedure
are separate tasks, both dependent on the move convention described in
[methods and search](frap-0005).}
